acement recommendation introduces a likely wrong electrical code and refrigerant-family rationale, undermining the brief’s explicit “PFJ-coded 230 V single-phase” requirement.  \n   Evidence: “The ZP series is the industry standard for R-410A/R-290 compatible scroll compressors; the PFV electrical code corresponds to 208/230V 1-phase 60Hz, which is widely used in 50Hz applications with appropriate derating or specific 50Hz variants like the ZP24K5E-PFJ”  \n   Root cause: Appendix supplier fallback / `g5_appendix_writer.py` generated an LLM “near match” instead of enforcing exact brief constraints.  \n   Fix: In `g5_appendix_writer.py`, change replacement ranking from “nearest catalog match” to “constraint-satisfying match only”; if no verified PFJ 50 Hz R290 compressor is found, output `MANUAL SOURCING REQUIRED` rather than `ZP24K5E-PFV-800`.\n\n3. (severity HIGH) — Axial Fan Impeller Drive / Appendix A  \n   Finding: The main fan prose respects the axial 450–500 mm >3000 m³/h brief, but Appendix A reintroduces a prohibited centrifugal fan recommendation.  \n   Evidence: “A ebm-papst axial fan blade (part HyBlade A5D500 family, 450-500 mm diameter) (additional: performance: e3,000 m³/h).” / “Recommended part number K3G500-AR02-01” / “The K3G500 series is the industry-standard 500mm EC centrifugal fan platform”  \n   Root cause: Stage 4.5 replacement resolver / `part_resolver/fan_candidates.py` searched by diameter and EC motor class but did not preserve fan topology.  \n   Fix: In `part_resolver/fan_candidates.py`, add `fan_type == \"axial\"` as a non-negotiable filter and reject candidate descriptions containing `centrifugal`, `blower`, or `backward-curved`; only allow ebm-papst AxiBlade/HyBlade axial SKUs with `diameter_mm between 450 and 500` and `airflow_m3h >= 3000`.\n\n4. (severity MED) — Axial Fan Impeller Drive / Fan Motor  \n   Finding: The fan is still not specified as a verified exact ebm-papst HyBlade A5D500 orderable assembly; the report uses “family” and an unverified motor code.  \n   Evidence: “A ebm-papst ec fan motor (part A5D500-A02” / “A search of the provided documents and general ebm-papst product naming conventions does not yield any evidence of a part number matching ‘A5D500-A02’.” / “A ebm-papst axial fan blade (part HyBlade A5D500 family”  \n   Root cause: Module synthesis / `stage_3_modules/component_writer.py` permitted family-level SKUs for a prescriptive brief.  \n   Fix: In `component_writer.py`, when `brief.prescriptive_part_required == true`, require `component.part_number_status == \"orderable_sku\"`; reject strings matching `family|series|platform`.\n\n5. (severity HIGH) — Hydronic Loop & Pump / Wetted materials  \n   Finding: The brief’s “HNBR/PTFE not EPDM” requirement was not enforced; EPDM remains in multiple wetted hydronic components and seals.  \n   Evidence: “carbon steel with EPDM membrane” / “stainless steel braid over EPDM core” / “4x Trelleborg hydronic pipe seal (part OR-28-3-EPDM, in EPDM 70 Shore A”  \n   Root cause: Materials compatibility checker / `constraints/material_compatibility.py` appears to have applied HNBR/PTFE only to refrigerant valves, not all wetted seals requested by the brief.  \n   Fix: In `material_compatibility.py`, implement `for part in bom if part.wetted == true and part.material contains \"EPDM\": raise GateFail(\"EPDM prohibited by brief\")`; replace expansion vessel membrane/flex hose/O-rings with HNBR, FKM, or PTFE-compatible alternatives depending on fluid exposure.\n\n6. (severity MED) — Safety Valves & Ignition Isolation  \n   Finding: Refrigerant-side valves mostly follow the HNBR/PTFE instruction, but the design is internally inconsistent because the same report also recommends EPDM replacements in Appendix A.  \n   Evidence: “A Sporlan electronic expansion valve (part SEH-100, in brass body, HNBR/PTFE seals” / “A Sanhua 4-way reversing valve (part SHF-9A-46, in brass body, PTFE seals” / “Recommended manufacturer Parker Hannifin Recommended part number 2-133-EPDM”  \n   Root cause: Appendix replacement generation / `g5_replacement_recommender.py` is not constraint-aware after initial component synthesis.  \n   Fix: Pass `brief.banned_materials=[\"EPDM\"]` into the replacement recommender and add `candidate.materials.intersection(banned_materials) == empty` before emitting any recommendation.\n\n7. (severity MED) — MCU Controller & Comms  \n   Finding: The controller family constraint was respected in intent—STM32F0/L0, not F407—but the BoM still uses a non-orderable family placeholder rather than a real MCU SKU in the main design.  \n   Evidence: “The main controller board is built around an STM32F0/L0 family MCU” / “A STMicroelectronics mcu pcb assembled (part STM32F0/L0 family” / “The provided part number ‘STM32F0/L0 family’ refers to a product series rather than a specific, orderable industrial part number.”  \n   Root cause: Controller part selection / `stage_3_modules/controller_selector.py` selected an MCU family but failed to bind it to an exact procurement SKU before BoM export.  \n   Fix: In `controller_selector.py`, resolve family to exact SKU before BoM generation, e.g. set `mcu.part_number = \"STM32F091RCT6\"` or another verified STM32F0/L0 device satisfying flash/RAM/I/O; fail G5 if `part_number` contains `/` or `family`.\n\n8. (severity HIGH) — G5 part numbers / Appendix A  \n   Finding: G5 jumped from 0 unverified to 81 because the stricter verifier now strips many fabricated or descriptive SKUs that earlier iterations apparently allowed through.  \n   Evidence: “81 part numbers could not be verified against DigiKey / Mouser / Farnell / web — manual sourcing required.” / “Total checked: 160 Verified: 56 Stripped (fakes): 81 Uncertain: 23” / “Stage 4.5 part-number verification did not find these SKUs at DigiKey, Mouser, Farnell, or via a Brave manufacturer-domain search.”  \n   Root cause: Stage 4.5 verification / `verify_parts.py` changed behavior to classify fabricated/descriptive SKUs as “stripped”, but the cover/BoM still counts them inconsistently as priced part lines.  \n   Fix: Split verification states in code: `custom_fab`, `verified_catalog`, `unverified_candidate`, `rejected_fake`; do not count `custom_fab` as G5 failure if a drawing/spec exists, but do count catalog-branded fake SKUs as failures. Update cover calculation to `unverified = unverified_candidate + rejected_fake`, not mixed totals.\n\n9. (severity HIGH) — Cost Stack / Bill of Materials  \n   Finding: The cost section is not credible and does not reconcile with the tracker’s £5,074 figure or the brief’s £1,800 OEM ceiling; it reports £2,044.77 raw BoM and £2,592.51 OEM transfer while relying heavily on web estimates and an aggressive scale factor.  \n   Evidence: “Raw materials BoM £2,044.77” / “= OEM transfer price £2,592.51” / “The architecture… meet a £1,800 OEM unit cost ceiling.” / “20 actual ~ 140 estimate ? 0 TBD” / “Raw BoM scale factor 0.325”  \n   Root cause: Costing stage / `stage_5_costing/cost_stack.py` applies optimistic batch economics to mostly unverified web-estimated lines and does not enforce the target as a design gate.  \n   Fix: In `cost_stack.py`, disallow “PASS” cost confidence unless `actual_or_supplier_quote_coverage >= 0.7`; apply scale factor only to verified distributor/OEM quotes; add `if oem_transfer_price > target_oem_cost * 1.15: gate=\"FAIL_COST\"`.\n\n10. (severity HIGH) — Cost reduction path  \n   Finding: To cut a real £5,074 BoM roughly in half, small component shaving will not work; the architecture must be simplified and supplier-quoted as an integrated refrigeration platform.  \n   Evidence: “Cost by module… Mass Fluid Transport Process £481.38… Energy Conversion Transduction £357.53… Safety Protection £424.29” / “20 actual ~ 140 estimate” / “custom-design single-phase inverter pcb” / “Dow potting compound… £236.33”  \n   Root cause: Architecture/cost optimizer / `design_optimizer/cost_reduction.py` did not run a target-cost trade study after the stricter brief.  \n   Fix: Add a cost-down pass that evaluates: remove custom inverter if compressor is fixed-speed PSC/approved OEM drive; source compressor+BPHE+EXV+reversing valve as an OEM refrigeration kit; consolidate duplicate hydronic valves/gauges/drains; replace industrial gas detector/relay board with appliance-grade certified R290 detector module; replace bespoke cabinet/acoustic parts with supplier DFM quote. Code: `run_costdown(target=1800, levers=[\"integrated_refrigeration_pack\",\"remove_duplicate_hydronics\",\"controller_hmi_integration\",\"supplier_quote_override\"])`.\n\n11. (severity HIGH) — Sensors & Leak Detection  \n   Finding: The R290 leak detector is mounted at the high point, which is a serious new engineering error because propane is heavier than air and leak detection should be placed near likely low accumulation points unless airflow analysis proves otherwise.  \n   Evidence: “R290 leak detector positioned at the compartment's high point.” / “mounted at the highest point of the compressor compartment.”  \n   Root cause: Safety synthesis / `stage_3_modules/safety_writer.py` used generic refrigerant-gas language instead of R290-specific density behavior.  \n   Fix: In `safety_writer.py`, add refrigerant property rule: `if refrigerant == \"R290\": gas_sensor.mounting = \"low point / below leak sources / drain path, validated by airflow CFD\"; prohibit \"high point\"`.\n\n12. (severity HIGH) — Appendix B / Physics critic  \n   Finding: A stale or wrong physics-critic result was carried into the final report, claiming hydronic isolation valves and expansion vessel are missing even though the generated modules include them.  \n   Evidence: “The brief requires the hydronic loop to include isolation valves at flow and return, and an expansion vessel >=18L. These components are completely missing from the provided modules.” / “A Zilmet hydronic expansion vessel (part CAL-PRO 18” / “A Pegler Yorkshire flow isolation valve… A Pegler Yorkshire return isolation valve”  \n   Root cause: Physics critic input assembly / `critics/physics_critic_payload.py` likely evaluated a truncated or earlier JSON payload rather than the rendered final module tree.  \n   Fix: Re-run critics only on `final_design.json` after all repair passes; add checksum binding: `critic_input_sha == rendered_report_design_sha`; suppress stale critic findings if the cited missing components are present in final BoM.\n\n13. (severity MED) — Brief & Requirements / Physical specification  \n   Finding: The physical envelope has an impossible EPAL footprint height of 144 mm for an 8 kW monobloc heat pump, likely a unit or parsing error introduced this iteration.  \n   Evidence: “Envelope footprint 1200 × 800 × 144 mm (L × W × H)”  \n   Root cause: Requirements parser / `brief_parser/envelope.py` appears to have imported pallet deck height or malformed dimensional data as product height.  \n   Fix: In `envelope.py`, validate heat-pump envelope with class bounds: `height_mm >= 600 and height_mm <= 1800`; if EPAL is selected, store pallet standard separately from product dimensions.\n\n14. (severity MED) — Regulatory & Compliance / Acoustics  \n   Finding: The report mixes two incompatible acoustic claims: 56 dBA at 1 m and MCS 020’s neighbour-noise calculation limit.  \n   Evidence: “MCS 020 sound power limit of 56 dBA at 1 metre” / “MCS 020 noise requirements for outdoor units.” / “MCS Planning Standard… d42 dBA at 1 m from neighbour.”  \n   Root cause: Compliance writer / `compliance/acoustics_rules.py` conflated sound power, sound pressure, and MCS 020 planning calculation outputs.  \n   Fix: Replace the hardcoded acoustic text with fields: `sound_power_LwA`, `sound_pressure_at_1m`, and `MCS020_at_neighbour`; require a calculator result rather than prose claim.\n\n15. (severity LOW) — Bill of Materials / Market comparison  \n   Finding: The cost benchmark text references a 30 kW monobloc even though the design is 8 kW, indicating copied or stale calibration text.  \n   Evidence: “Installed ASP for 30 kW R290 monobloc: hardware (~£9-13k) + scaled pipework + electrics + MCS commissioning = £18-27k total” / “Design thermal output 8 kW”  \n   Root cause: Cost benchmark renderer / `costing/market_benchmark_text.py` reused a benchmark paragraph from a different capacity class.  \n   Fix: Parameterize benchmark text by `design_thermal_output_kw`; for this report emit only 8 kW domestic ASHP comparables and fail if paragraph contains `30 kW` when `design_kw != 30`.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Drafting findings**\n\nI need to provide pseudo code in the same format as requested. It sounds like I also need to include a verbatim quote from a PDF, so I should find that relevant citation. They want about 12 findings, and it’s important to include each question along with the severity. I might organize this in a clear way to make sure the findings are easily understandable and address everything they’ve asked for.**Clarifying recommendations**\n\nI'm noticing that the resolver isn't able to recommend centrifugal options when the brief specifies axial. I might need to reassess my approach here. Also, it looks like I need to use (d) instead of F407. I wonder if I’m overlooking any details in the specifications. Maybe I should double-check to ensure everything aligns correctly. Getting this right is important to meet the user’s needs, so I'll adjust accordingly!","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.summary","summary":"**Drafting findings**\n\nI need to provide pseudo code in the same format as requested. It sounds like I also need to include a verbatim quote from a PDF, so I should find that relevant citation. They want about 12 findings, and it’s important to include each question along with the severity. I might organize this in a clear way to make sure the findings are easily understandable and address everything they’ve asked for.**Clarifying recommendations**\n\nI'm noticing that the resolver isn't able t